{"name":"Canterbury Cathedral","hex":"#b2ab94","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"canterbury","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rack for magazines or other papers, usually wooden, often done as a decorative piece."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/canterbury"},{"term":"cathedral","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Relating to the office of a bishop or an archbishop."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The principal church serving as the office (and some as place of residence) of an archdiocese's/a diocese's archbishop/bishop which is symbolized by an episcopal throne known as the cathedra.","A large or important church building."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cathedral"}],"entries":[{"title":"Canterbury Cathedral","blocks":[{"text":"Canterbury Cathedral is the cathedral of the archbishop of Canterbury, the spiritual leader of the Church of England and symbolic leader of the worldwide Anglican Communion. Located in Canterbury, Kent, it is on the site of one of the oldest Christian structures in England and forms part of a World Heritage Site. Its formal title is the Cathedral and Metropolitical Church of Christ, Canterbury."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Canterbury_Cathedral_evening_%284902497028%29.jpg/330px-Canterbury_Cathedral_evening_%284902497028%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canterbury_Cathedral"},{"title":"Canterbury","blocks":[{"text":"Canterbury is a city and UNESCO World Heritage Site in the Canterbury district, county of Kent, England; it was a county borough until 1974. Lying on the River Stour not far from its mouth on the Strait of Dover, the city has a mild oceanic climate."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Canterbury-cathedral-wyrdlight.jpg/330px-Canterbury-cathedral-wyrdlight.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canterbury"},{"title":"Cathedral","blocks":[{"text":"A cathedral is a church that contains the cathedra of a bishop, thus serving as the central church of a diocese, conference, or episcopate. Churches with the function of \"cathedral\" are usually specific to those Christian denominations with an episcopal hierarchy, such as the Catholic, Eastern Orthodox, Anglican, and some Lutheran churches. Church buildings embodying the functions of a cathedral first appeared in Italy, Gaul, Spain, and North Africa in the 4th century, but cathedrals did not become universal within the Western Catholic Church until the 12th century, by which time they had developed architectural forms, institutional structures, and legal identities distinct from parish churches, monastic churches, and episcopal residences. The cathedral is more important in the hierarchy than the church because it is from the cathedral that the bishop governs the area under their administrative authority."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Catedral_da_S%C3%A9_em_S%C3%A3o_Paulo.jpg/330px-Catedral_da_S%C3%A9_em_S%C3%A3o_Paulo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cathedral"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Augustine_of_Canterbury_sculpture_on_Canterbury_Cathedral.jpg/500px-Augustine_of_Canterbury_sculpture_on_Canterbury_Cathedral.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Augustine_of_Canterbury_sculpture_on_Canterbury_Cathedral.jpg/1280px-Augustine_of_Canterbury_sculpture_on_Canterbury_Cathedral.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Augustine_of_Canterbury_sculpture_on_Canterbury_Cathedral.jpg/500px-Augustine_of_Canterbury_sculpture_on_Canterbury_Cathedral.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAugustine_of_Canterbury_sculpture_on_Canterbury_Cathedral.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/St_Augustine%27s_Abbey_with_Canterbury_Cathedral_in_fog_2024-12-29.jpg/500px-St_Augustine%27s_Abbey_with_Canterbury_Cathedral_in_fog_2024-12-29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/St_Augustine%27s_Abbey_with_Canterbury_Cathedral_in_fog_2024-12-29.jpg/1280px-St_Augustine%27s_Abbey_with_Canterbury_Cathedral_in_fog_2024-12-29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/St_Augustine%27s_Abbey_with_Canterbury_Cathedral_in_fog_2024-12-29.jpg/500px-St_Augustine%27s_Abbey_with_Canterbury_Cathedral_in_fog_2024-12-29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASt_Augustine's_Abbey_with_Canterbury_Cathedral_in_fog_2024-12-29.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Canterburycathedralthrone.jpg/500px-Canterburycathedralthrone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Canterburycathedralthrone.jpg/1280px-Canterburycathedralthrone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Canterburycathedralthrone.jpg/500px-Canterburycathedralthrone.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The archiepiscopal throne in Canterbury Cathedral","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanterburycathedralthrone.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wi